Let $M$ be a smooth compact Riemannian manifold with boundary $\partial M$, regarded as the spatial domain occupied by an ideal (incompressible, inviscid) fluid. Assume that a (possibly time-dependent) normal velocity profile is prescribed on $\partial M$, modeling sources (regions where fluid enters $M$) and sinks (regions where it leaves). A standard example is a pipe $M$ with prescribed nonzero inflow on one boundary component and outflow on another.

In the case of closed $M$ (or $M$ with impermeable boundary, i.e.\ vanishing normal velocity), the motion of the fluid is described by Euler's equations
\[
\partial_t v + \nabla_v v = -\nabla p, \qquad \mathrm{div}\,v = 0,
\]
and admits a well-known geodesic (variational) formulation due to Arnold: solutions correspond to geodesics of the right-invariant $L^2$ metric on the group $\mathrm{Diff}_\mu(M)$ of volume-preserving diffeomorphisms of $M$.

Problem. Give a variational, and preferably geodesic, formulation of the motion of an ideal incompressible inviscid fluid in $M$ when the boundary $\partial M$ carries nontrivial sources and sinks. Specify precisely:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the configuration space (e.g.\ an appropriate space or groupoid of diffeomorphisms or transport maps of $M$ compatible with the prescribed boundary flux);
\item the Lagrangian or Riemannian/sub-Riemannian structure whose critical points (resp.\ geodesics) reproduce the Euler equations with the given boundary data;
\item which auxiliary data must enter the formulation, such as the prescribed boundary fluxes, exterior forces, or additional "memory" variables recording the history of fluid particles that have entered through sources.
\end{enumerate}
The formulation must include, as concrete examples, the case of a pipe-shaped domain $M$ with prescribed nonzero inflow and outflow on disjoint parts of $\partial M$.

